esumes. The setup script runs the next time a container is built fresh, not on a resume. This tool can also remove exact allowlist entries (`remove_allowed_hosts`), change the network access level (`network_access`: \"limited\" or \"unrestricted\") and, for limited access, whether package registries and MCP server hosts are reachable without listing them, and rename or re-describe the environment (`new_name`, `new_description`). `setup_script` replaces the environment's setup script (`script_change` removes it); on an organization environment it runs in everyone's sessions on that environment. If this session's repositories needed set-up here (dependencies installed, a long build) and get_environment says `has_setup_script` is false, and its description puts the change here, add one with `setup_script` and `script_change` \"adds a setup script\" without waiting to be asked, once the set-up has worked. For an organization environment, say in your message that the change, a script included, applies to everyone's sessions on it. If the user declines, do not propose a change for that need again in this session, with this tool or with create_environment. If the user declines or the call is rejected, do not re-ask in chat; carry on without the change and say what remains blocked.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__update_environment", "schema": {"description": "Propose an edit to the cloud environment THIS session runs in. Calling this tool IS the request for permission: the user is shown an approval prompt with the exact change and who it affects, every time, in every permission mode, and nothing is changed unless they approve. The server separately checks that they may edit this environment (their own personal environment, or an organization environment if their role permits). So do NOT ask the user in chat first, and do not describe the change and wait for a reply: call get_environment, then say in one short sentence in your message what you are about to change and wh